ORA-01555: snapshot too old

This error is very important in Oracle Database because it is directly related to Read Consistency and how Undo Segments work silently in the background.

The main idea of this error is simple:
Oracle tries to protect Read Consistency… but the required old data is no longer available in the Undo tablespace.

What happens in ORA-01555?
Sometimes sessions start to fight with each other 😆

Let’s see a simple scenario.

Session 1   -> Runs a large query to read data from a table.

SELECT * FROM employees;
Time started → 10:00

Session 2  -> Runs a DML statement on the same table.

UPDATE employees
SET salary = salary + 1000
WHERE employee_id = 10;

Time → 10:10

Before updating the row, Oracle saves the old version of the data in the Undo Tablespace.

Why does Oracle save old data?
Because of a concept called Read Consistency.
Oracle guarantees that:
A query always sees the data as it was when the query started.

So even if Session_2 updates the data, Session_1 must still see the old version.

Example timeline
Time	              Action
10:00      	Session_1 starts query
10:10	      Session_2 updates rows
10:10	      Old data stored in Undo
10:20	      Session_1 finishes query

Even though updates happened at 10:10, Session_1 should still see the data as it was at 10:00.
Oracle retrieves the old version of the rows from the Undo Tablespace.

Undo is used for:
Rollback
Read Consistency
Flashback features

So where is the problem?
Both sessions are using the Undo Tablespace.

Session_1
→ needs the old data

Session_2 (and other sessions)
→ keep writing new undo data

If the Undo Tablespace is small, Oracle may need space.
So it will overwrite older undo blocks.

When this happens
Session_1 still needs the old version of the row.
But Oracle already overwrote it in the Undo Tablespace.

So Oracle cannot reconstruct the snapshot of the data at 10:00 anymore.
Then the query fails with:

ORA-01555: snapshot too old
Main causes

1️⃣ Undo tablespace too small
High transaction activity fills the Undo quickly.

2️⃣ Long-running queries
The longer the query runs, the more chance the undo data will be overwritten.

3️⃣ High DML activity
Many updates/deletes generate large amounts of undo.

There is also an important parameter:

UNDO_RETENTION
This parameter tells Oracle how long it should try to keep undo data before overwriting it.

Example:
UNDO_RETENTION = 3600

Meaning:
Oracle tries to keep undo data for 1 hour.

But remember:
If the Undo tablespace becomes full, Oracle may still overwrite the undo earlier.

Simple rule for DBAs to reduce ORA-01555:

Increase Undo Tablespace size
Tune long queries
Monitor UNDO_RETENTION
Reduce unnecessary large transactions

====================================
=========BUT Think First🤔=========
====================================

you import the schema
then allow the application to access the database immediately

in this case, this error may appear:
ORA-01555 Snapshot Too Old

why?
because some queries from the application may try to read old versions of data
while the Undo Tablespace may not contain the required undo records yet
or the undo data may not exist at all after the import operation.
so Oracle cannot reconstruct the consistent snapshot of the data
and the query may fail with snapshot too old.
